divergence-scalar-times-vector.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit where the scalar field \b{f} approaches a constant value, say \b{c}, its gradient \b{ 
abla f} becomes zero. In this case, the identity simplifies to \b{ 
abla · (c →A) = c ( 
abla · →A)}, which is the expected linearity property of the divergence operator. Conversely, if the vector field \b{→A} is irrotational (i.e., \b{ 
abla · →A = 0}), the divergence of the product becomes entirely due to the scalar field's gradient: \b{ 
abla · (f →A) = →A · ( 
abla f)}.
